---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { publications } from '../data/site';

const realityGapPaper = publications.find((p) => p.title.startsWith('A Multi-Modality Evaluation'))!;
const perturbationBenchmarkPaper = publications.find((p) => p.title.startsWith('Benchmarking Image Perturbations'))!;
const perturbationDriveJournalPaper = publications.find((p) => p.title.startsWith('PerturbationDrive:'))!;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Teaching — Stefano Carlo Lambertenghi"
  description="A practicum course on testing autonomous driving systems, and the student projects that have grown out of my research."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-inner container-wide">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Teaching / students and projects</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">Teaching.</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="page-hero-copy">
        I work on the reality gap, under the supervision of Prof. Andrea Stocco, and much of
        that work happens together with students. This page collects the practicum course and
        the student projects that have grown out of it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="practicum-heading">
    <div class="container-wide demo-showcase">
      <div class="video-frame">
        <iframe
          src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/Q8-Wu7h2mlo"
          title="ADS testing practicum final presentations"
          allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          allowfullscreen
          loading="lazy"
        ></iframe>
      </div>
      <div class="demo-showcase-copy">
        <p class="feature-kicker">Practicum</p>
        <h2 id="practicum-heading">A practicum on testing autonomous driving.</h2>
        <p>
          Together with my colleagues Xingcheng Chen and Lev Sorokin, and under the supervision
          of Andrea Stocco, I helped organize a practicum course on testing automated driving
          systems. Students worked in small, mentored teams, applying automated testing
          techniques—both in simulation and on small-scale physical vehicles—to systems such as a
          lane-keeping controller based on a deep neural network. The course ended with a final
          event where each team presented its prototype.
        </p>
        <p>
          That edition's two groups worked on opposite sides of the same problem. One used
          generative models to translate simulated camera images into realistic ones in real
          time, so a model trained on real data could still drive in simulation. The other used
          domain-augmentation techniques during training, so a model trained only in simulation
          could hold up when tested in the real world.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="people-heading">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-heading" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Not a one-person project</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="people-heading" class="display-title">Student projects.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Some of the student projects that have grown out of this work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="people-grid">
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo" style="aspect-ratio: 1200 / 1135;">
            <img
              src="/images/davide-xun.jpg"
              alt="Davide Jannussi and Xun Ma next to Fortuna"
              width="1200"
              height="1135"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Master’s students</p>
            <h3>Davide Jannussi &amp; Xun Ma</h3>
            <p>
              Davide built Cam2Sim, turning our recorded drives into playable, closed-loop CARLA
              scenarios reconstructed with Gaussian Splatting. Xun’s thesis studied how to adapt
              a model trained purely in simulation so it holds up when tested in the real world.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04770" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Read Davide’s paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo">
            <img
              src="/images/matthias-andrea-me.jpg"
              alt="Matthias Weil, Andrea Stocco, and Stefano Carlo Lambertenghi next to Fortuna"
              width="1400"
              height="1050"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Bachelor’s thesis</p>
            <h3>Matthias Weil</h3>
            <p>
              As a bachelor’s student, Matthias co-led the real-world perturbation study that
              became our ASE 2026 paper: 72 camera and LiDAR perturbations compared across
              offline analysis, hardware-in-the-loop execution, and 740+ closed-loop runs on the
              physical vehicle.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04953" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Read the paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo" style="aspect-ratio: 1920 / 1080;">
            <img
              src="/images/mirena-work.jpg"
              alt="Mirena Flores Valdez, and a split view comparing the real fortiss Labs test room with its simulated digital twin"
              width="1920"
              height="1080"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Master’s thesis</p>
            <h3>Mirena Flores Valdez</h3>
            <p>
              Her master's thesis became the ASE 2025 study comparing simulation,
              vehicle-in-the-loop, and mixed-reality testing on the small-scale platform.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href={realityGapPaper.paper} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Read the paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo">
            <img
              src="/images/skander.jpg"
              alt="Skander Jneyeh presenting his bachelor's thesis alongside Stefano Carlo Lambertenghi and Andrea Stocco"
              width="1400"
              height="1050"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Bachelor’s thesis</p>
            <h3>Skander Jneyeh</h3>
            <p>
              For his bachelor’s thesis, Skander built a physical, IoT-connected traffic-light
              system for the small-scale platform, so the vehicle can detect and react to traffic
              lights through camera perception or direct communication.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo" style="aspect-ratio: 1487 / 910;">
            <img
              src="/images/hannes-thesis.jpg"
              alt="Hannes Leonhard, and a diagram of PerturbationDrive applying motion blur to a camera feed before it reaches the driving model"
              width="1487"
              height="910"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Bachelor’s thesis</p>
            <h3>Hannes Leonhard</h3>
            <p>
              As a bachelor's student, Hannes built PerturbationDrive, the image-perturbation
              framework for testing automated driving assistance systems. His thesis was the
              basis for both the framework and the ICST 2025 paper benchmarking it.
            </p>
            <div class="contributor-links">
              <a class="text-link" href={perturbationDriveJournalPaper.paper} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                Journal paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
              </a>
              <a class="text-link" href={perturbationBenchmarkPaper.paper} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                ICST 2025 paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="person-card" data-reveal>
          <div class="person-photo" style="aspect-ratio: 1118 / 669;">
            <img
              src="/images/constantin-thesis.jpg"
              alt="Constantin Carste, next to CARLA reconstructions and photos of the Tesla Model 3 and Fortuna used to record real driving data"
              width="1118"
              height="669"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="person-body">
            <p class="person-role">Bachelor’s thesis</p>
            <h3>Constantin Carste</h3>
            <p>
              Constantin's bachelor's thesis used Stable Diffusion and CARLA to reconstruct real
              driving scenarios and automatically generate matching road layouts from recordings
              taken on a Tesla Model 3 and on Fortuna—the basis Cam2Sim was later built on.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04770" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Read the paper <span aria-hidden="true">↗︎</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <ul class="contributor-list" aria-label="More collaborators">
        <li class="contributor-row" data-reveal>
          <div>
            <h3>Timo Götz</h3>
            <p>
              Timo's industrial thesis, done in collaboration with BMW, automatically validates
              acoustic test-bench recordings of electric motors—separating whether a test ran the
              right physical scenario from whether the recorded signal itself is trustworthy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="contributor-row" data-reveal>
          <div>
            <h3>Gökce Sengün</h3>
            <p>
              Gökce's thesis extends the ICST 2024 quality-metrics study to system-level testing:
              instead of checking offline model accuracy alone, it tests whether the same
              image-quality metrics also predict how the driving system behaves on a physical
              vehicle.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
